$DIR/main.p:27:2: cannot pass arr[:] to each, which keeps it after returning
    arr[:] points into t. t is a local variable of f (declared at main.p:25).
    each passes a pointer loaded from ks[i] to k.Keep (which may call
    main.(*T).Keep) (main.p:19), which stores t in package variable keep
    (main.p:15), so arr[:] would outlive t.
    Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free
    it later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the
    heap behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the
    code.
    If each should keep its own copy of t, change it to take the value, not a
    pointer, or an owner it can keep: an owned[T] from new, a shared[T], or a
    library owner such as a vec.Vec.
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3003" for a longer explanation)
